        cause: 'Sobibór is the least documented of the Reinhard camps, and the revolt is one of the few episodes with sources numerous enough to be cross-examined. The planning joined two prisoner groups with little in common: Feldhendler’s Polish Jews, who had been in the camp since 1942 and knew its routines, and the Soviet Jewish prisoners of war who arrived in September 1943 and spoke Russian rather than Yiddish or Polish. Pechersky brought military method - compartmented planning, a signal, a reserve group - and Feldhendler brought knowledge of the camp.',
        what: 'The plan was not a rush at the fence but a decapitation of the Vorlager: kill the guards in the administrative area, take the armoury, cut the telephone, then walk 600 prisoners out through the main gate on the evening roll call. It failed at the second step, because the rifles could not all be gathered and the alarm was raised with only eleven SS men dead; the breakout became an improvised run through a minefield, partly along the lane the Germans used themselves. Postwar testimony differs over the armoury and over Pechersky’s decision to leave with the Soviet group, and Jules Schelvis’s reconstruction from transport records now underpins the numbers.',
        consequence: 'The revolt is read as a moral event rather than a military one, and its measurable effect was German policy: Himmler’s answer was the Harvest Festival shootings of 3 and 4 November 1943, which killed the surviving Jewish forced labourers of the district. For the history of the Holocaust the site matters twice over - as a killing centre of which almost no physical trace was left, because the SS ploughed and wooded it, and as a case where perpetrator records and survivor memory had to be reconciled by demographers working from transport lists.',
